12:33:35 [autopublish.editor] Claude call failed: Command '['claude', '-p', 'You are the copy editor for "letters from the abysmal." You have ONE job: fix clear mechanical errors. That\'s it.\n\nRules:\n- Fix typos (regtrettably → regrettably, I#m → I\'m)\n- Fix broken punctuation (missing periods, unclosed quotes)\n- Fix genuinely unclear referents ONLY if the meaning is truly ambiguous\n- DO NOT rewrite anything\n- DO NOT smooth transitions\n- DO NOT make the tone "nicer" or more conventional\n- DO NOT add or remove emphasis\n- DO NOT restructure paragraphs\n- DO NOT add headers or formatting\n- Preserve ALL of Esther\'s stylistic choices: sentence fragments, em dashes, parenthetical asides, unconventional punctuation, run-on thoughts, everything\n\nThis is Esther\'s voice. Here\'s a reference:\n\n<reference>\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en">\n<head>\n<meta charset="UTF-8">\n<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">\n\n<title>Resistance Thoughts — from the abysmal</title>\n<link rel="canonical" href="https://fromtheabysmal.net/posts/2026-02-06-resistance-thoughts.html">\n<meta property="og:title" content="Resistance Thoughts">\n<meta property="og:description" content="Maybe Stephen Pressfield got it wrong. Maybe what we view as Resistance isn’t always that. Maybe, sometimes, it’s not the time to write/create/do, especially when we tell ourselves it is. On the">\n<meta property="og:url" content="https://fromtheabysmal.net/posts/2026-02-06-resistance-thoughts.html">\n<meta property="og:type" content="article">\n<link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css">\n<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="from the abysmal" href="https://fromtheabysmal.net/rss.xml">\n</head>\n<body>\n<header class="site-header">\n<a href="/" class="site-title-link" id="site-title">\n<img src="/title.png" alt="letters from the abysmal" class="site-title-img"\n     onerror="this.style.display=\'none\'; document.getElementById(\'site-title-text\').style.display=\'block\';">\n<h1 id="site-title-text" class="site-title-text" style="display:none;">from the abysmal</h1>\n</a>\n</header>\n<nav class="post-nav"><a href="/">← back to main</a></nav>\n<article class="post post-single">\n<div class="post-heading">\n<h1>Resistance Thoughts</h1>\n<div class="post-meta">February 6, 2026</div>\n</div>\n<div class="post-body">\n<p>Maybe Stephen Pressfield got it wrong. Maybe what we view as Resistance isn’t always that. Maybe, sometimes, it’s not the time to write/create/do, especially when we tell ourselves it is.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, I haven’t read The War of Art in a long time. Maybe I’m getting some stuff mixed up.</p>\n<p>That said, my experiment is going swimmingly — if the goal of making my computer unavailable for anything other than writing until noon every weekday is to make me... happier? I mean, my mind would <em>like</em> me to use every minute of those allotted two-hundred-forty to write and write and write down every. single. thought. that has ever occurred to me. It doesn’t work that way though, regrettably.</p>\n<p>But here’s a thought that occurred to me, about thoughts that occur — on my way back from my walk an hour ago, my mind was thinking something along the lines of “damn I am a good catch, Kiryll made a good choice,” and immediately flagged it as out of the ordinary, realizing, <em>huh, I am extremely ovulatory right now—</em></p>\n<p>So I was watching myself looking at myself from the inside going <em>DAMN,</em> and then watched myself put two and two together— <em>OH, and when I’m luteal and my body does not currently have the energy nor the need to attract a male (the opposite, even, because the baby box will be shedding its lining momentarily), my confidence goes down like the Titanic <strong>explicitly so that</strong> I will curl up in a ball and be alone and properly rest so my body can do the shedding of the baby box lining properly??</em></p>\n<p>Trains of thought such as these make me think thoughts do not belong in the realm of “things I should identify as originating from me, myself, or whatever I am” (I just listened to a good chunk of <em>Why Buddhism Is True</em>; it’s ding-ding-dinging non-stop lately in my brain, especially the significant overlap of principles and terminology between Buddhism and Human Design.)</p>\n<p>One of manifold reasons I love my Claude Code publishing pipeline<sup><a href="#fn1" id="fnr1">1</a></sup> choosing pieces to publish in my stead (instead of me making myself hone in on one piece to make it ready for publishing and forgetting all my joy of anything in the process and then leaving it to rot in the end after all because everything is cringe and none of my effort deserve to be seen even by a bot) is that I get to feel like I’m writing letters to “the outside” from a romantically forsaken and plant-kingdom-reclaimed abandoned bunker warehouse, and the words I’m typing into my shiny wonderful MacBook Air, are, like, relics of a forgotten time</p>\n<p>Yes, <em>maybe</em> the fact that I pulled out a bunch of ingredients earlier to make into something edible and also tasty so that my dad won’t feel like his weekly grocery hauls are in vain, in fact, points to my Resisting towards writing — <em>maybe</em> it also means that functionally disabling my computer till noon every weekday frees me up to actually register stuff that would otherwise get neglected because getting sucked into the internet early in the day is very bad bad bad bad bad bad bad bad bad for my soul.</p>\n<p>Funnily enough though, here I am, writing, yes? Beats me how any of this works. No, actually I really would like to explain it to myself in terms of the Rave I’Ching etc but I sadly do not have the time. I need to go make an herby baked cheesecake omelette type situation with olives, grated tomatoes and Vichy carrots. Goodbye now.</p>\n<div class="footnotes">\n<ol>\n<li id="fn1"><p>Edited; when I wrote this back in February, it was “kiryll publishing pieces in my stead,” which did not happen, but the principle remains <a href="#fnr1">↩︎</a></p></li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p class="post-history">Last revised April 27, 2026.</p>\n</div>\n<nav class="post-nav post-nav-bottom"><a href="/">← back to main</a></nav>\n</article>\n<hr class="section-rule">\n<footer class="site-footer">\n<span>Copyright © Esther Olschowy 2026 · Bergisch Gladbach, Germany</span>\n</footer>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n</reference>\n\nHere\'s the piece to copy-edit:\n\n<draft>\nv2\n\n\nHello. It’s been a long week of once again rethinking my approach to writing — writing on the internet yes or no; writing, why do it at all; writing, why this way and not that; writing with an agenda and without, and if yes, disclosing the agenda or not, and what kind of agenda?\n\nUltimately, I couldn’t relieve the pressure, and my frustration around this was both cause and effect. It was my birthday on Tuesday, and despite knowing things tend to come to a halt during this time of year — Gate 56, my natal Sun, is a stop codon after all — I was dead-set, almost childishly so, on writing, finishing, and publishing this piece before the event (and, to be honest, ideally also five others). \n\nAll the more unsurprising that, then, things got in the way — my cycle, my kid, the melancholy that generally arises alongside another circle round the sun drawing to a close, the weight of untold stories. The truth is that birthdays are both the best time of year to cultivate an open mind around the thing that makes you you — and to notice transit propaganda in its most insidious form: the need, want, the desire to “just be yourself.”\n\n---\n\nGenerally speaking, I don’t like birthdays. I’ve never loved gatherings, which. Plus, I’m ashamed to admit that somewhere deep down, I kinda disagree with the concept of celebrating myself for being born. It’s very undefined ego of me; I know it’s silly. After all, celebrating oneself despite having “done” nothing to merit said celebrating is, in the end, pretty damn life-affirming, an amazing antidote to the undefined ego compulsion to prove goshdarn everything, especially one’s right to exist. \n\nSo yeah, there’s one problem right there. Another one is, specifically for me, that my natal Sun is conjunct my Chiron, meaning that many a year on my birthday, the transiting Sun is passing through le natal Chiron, and this is quite simply not fun, not fun at all.\n\nBut what I’m really here to talk about is this heightened pressure to package *all* observation into neat pretty packages — that I kinda collapsed under while the Sun was transiting the fifty-six.\n\n\n\nBecause obviously one must always sprinkle in some good old fashioned hypocrisy with undefined ego things, that is simply a requirement\n\n\n\nIf you’re interested in an excessively detailed explanation, here we go:\n\nMy natal Chiron (56.4) is in the place where Stimulation becomes transpersonal and opportunistic, where it needs closeness, the baring of one’s soul to trusted others in confidence. \n\nThe fulfillment of this need has historically been unavailable to me. My design is hilariously fourth-line-free and my (third line) bonding strategy feels like it’s always been much more about breaking (interpersonal) bonds than making them in the first place. \n\n(This, I feel, has much to do with the maturation process contained in the combination of being undefined in the ego, spleen, and solar plex. I have a tendency to hold on to things that aren’t good for me — on top of avoiding confrontation, on top of feeling unworthy of letting go of what isn’t good for me, seeking out what is, and confronting both the latter and the former. Sometimes I am aware of this, and my brain, ever the good girl, then tends to overshoot in the opposite direction. Plus, with my Sun being in the line of Alienation, and my throat defined by the line of Selectivity, my design is just fucking extraordinarily picky in general.)\n\nErgo, the physical reality of birthdays for me nearly always defaults to feeling caught between either isolating myself completely, or accepting those who are available on my birthday (often immediate family) as confidantes, while not trusting them to accept me as I am for reasons I believe almost everyone with an immediate family can understand. So, seeing as the pressure on the day is Expediency, or stimulating expression in confidence, then, well, lol. The result is that I’m forced to reckon with feeling like I have never gotten, presently cannot and will in the future never receive what feels more important  than anything else, ever on this day, alongside the omnipresent hurt that comes with the thing that makes me most alive being tied up in the hurt of feeling forever unrecognized by those that supposedly matter most.\n\nI can hardly emphasize enough, though, that all of this is what I feel. The reality is more often than not a lot simpler than the mind makes it out to be. \n\n---\nv1\n\nbirthday suck, birthdays are the best\n\n\nHello. It’s been a long week of once again rethinking my entire approach writing on the internet — this time all the while observing a nonstop rush of words pushing to be released. Ultimately, I couldn’t relieve the pressure, and I’m unsure whether my frustration around this was the cause or the effect. Probably both, seeing as it was my birthday on Tuesday, and things always seem to come to a halt at this time of year. Gate 56 is a stop codon, after all.\n\nSince Saturday, I’ve been longing to finish this piece. Things got in the way: low energy due to my cycle, low energy due to my kid’s constant presence alongside my recently renewed determination to be *truly present*with him, low energy due to the melancholy that arises alongside the completion of another solar cycle, low energy due to the constantly accumulating weight of needing to tell.the.motherfucking.stories.fucking.pronto.\n\nNo wonder. Once again, it turns out that when I feel even slight pressure to do something — even when it feels like it is me, myself, who wants to do it — especially then — as is often the case when this pressure strangely enough lines up with what the transiting Sun is currently propagating — I cannot do it. \n\nThis hurts, like my own body is betraying me. ^1\n\nMind, under the influence, is a child who didn’t get the ice cream cone his mother has been stating all along he wouldn’t be getting today. ^2 Mind, here,  understands why it can’t have what it purports to want — and wants it even more. A body refusing to move for mind’s begs to conform? Yeah, that feels like a problem. Of course it leads to a person at war with herself.\n\n---\n\nGenerally speaking, I’m not a fan of birthdays. I’ve never loved gatherings as a general concept. One of the embarrassing undefined ego things I’m not a fan of, but that, deep down, are there, and I gotta deal with them, and my fingers are crossed that someday, they will transform into wisdom, but right now they’re just kinda stupid — is that I disagree with the concept of celebrating myself ^3 — but not others — for having been born. ^4\n\nAnyway, one of the reasons my birthday feels particularly un-fun for me and people around me — if not THE one reason — is that nearly every year on this exact day, the transiting Sun passes through my natal Chiron. My Solar Return (56.3 — Alienation, the result of trial and error in Stimulation) usually takes place the day before my birthday, so that, on the day of, the Sun has moved onto 56.4 — the place where Stimulation becomes transpersonal and opportunistic, where it needs closeness and the baring of one’s soul to another in confidence. But, that has historically been unavailable to me. My design is otherwise hilariously fourth-line-free and my bonding strategy has always seemed off-kilter, like it’s more about breaking bonds than making them in the first place. Ergo, I often default to accepting those who are available to me on my birthday (often immediate family) as the confidantes I seem to need, but also don’t actually want them as confidantes because, well, the aforementioned group dynamics make my throat close up, and if the pressure on the day is expression in confidence, then, well, lol. The result is  being forced to reckon with not getting what my mind feels like it needs more than anything ever on this day, and the always-present hurt that comes with the thing that makes me most alive — wandering in every sense — being tied up with this wound of both feeling chronically, forever unrecognized by those that supposedly matter most.\n\nI’ve come to accept that there’s virtually nothing I can do to sidestep the bitter ache that will be present on my birthday, as surely as the planets move. There is always something. But this is the quintessential self-fulfilling prophecy, mind says. You need to embrace the disappointment, or you need to stop with all the expectations, or you need to just not be disappointed and bitter. \n\nAt the same time, it languishes in the self-accusations that follow, repeating ad nauseam that you’re such an ungrateful, self-absorbed, touchy little princess, you always make it about your silly little needs, you engineer this same predicament every single year by making sure your loved ones cannot help but let you down — and then you complain about feeling misunderstood.\n\nA case of life sucks, then you die, I guess.\n\n---\n\nIf you’re wondering now, my birthday did in fact turn out to be a good ol’ bottle of Emotional Growth Juice previously buried under Emotional Inconvenience topsoil. And, someone close to me — a confidant — irony knows no bounds — did end up remarking to my carefully expressing how I would rather be alone on this day because the shit will happen no matter what I do and, this way, at least, I won’t pull others into the mess: come on, that’s such a self-fulfilling prophecy. I said: I know, but that’s just how it is right now. I don’t like it, but it also just feels too much like throwing myself from the car onto the highway, at night. I would like to wait for the car to slow down a little first.\n\nI’m not very experienced with the intricacies of knowing “the cause” — this Chiron-Sun conjunction —  yet, having lived with the results of the going into the same mental loops every year without fail. Those neural pathways are hard to un-tread. \n\nThe best solution I’ve come up with is to draw the boundaries as absolutes. The year before last, I announced beforehand that I would be reading all day, and did so. It was amazing. Nourishing, relaxed, wonderful. Next year, I plan to plan a solo getaway months beforehand, being absolutely clear that I will not interact with anyone close to me in-person, at all, on the day of. Maybe that’s also just avoidance and will backfire like this year, but then again, this year I simply wasn’t clear enough both with myself and those close to me what “I want to be alone” actually entails.\n\n---\n\nJust last week, I was sitting on a cute *nine* half-finished drafts for this newsletter. That number fluctuates often, of course, but not by much. As of Monday, I’ve had more than twenty drafts. *Eleven* of them were begun over the last few days, and they don’t even represent everything I wanted to write down.\n\nFor me, drawing conclusions from stories and seeing the hidden meaning in every experience is not pathological, they are core to who I am. Yet these tendencies are among those my mind judges about me and itself most harshly. \n\nYes, sometimes things that happen don’t mean anything except that life sucks. Sometimes there’s no lesson to be learned, just a scar — in these cases, if there is a lesson in the end, the act of looking for it will definitely help not in finding it but in the moral of the story eluding your grasp like wet algae.\n\nPaying special attention to the pressure our minds exert upon us when it’s that time of year can clue us in on what they may be failing to notice about our being the rest of the time, thereby either consciously or at least semi-consciously suppressing these vital parts of our beings.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n1. There’s been a lot of that lately — this has to be its own letter. Feeling betrayed by your body can be both an uncomfortable reality of deconditioning and/or a sign that your mind’s wants or goals are incompatible with your body’s capabilities.\n\n2. He noticed some people holding one on the way back from the playground, and his mind latched onto hope despite his mother’s firmness, the possibility became indistinguishable from the current reality, and the letdown when they passed the ice cream place without going inside was so fierce that all the consolation and explanations couldn’t put his shattered mood together again. \n\nI hope it’s obvious that I speak from experience, from all possible vantage points in this image, literal and metaphorical. The mother is both real and a symbol of the well-meaning, “rational” but often dense voice in our heads that thinks it can simply decide what happens without understanding the underlying mechanisms behind the “irrational” kid voice that will not “simply obey” — because it cannot.\n\nMaybe the mother’s well-meaning attempt to shut down false hope fueled the kid’s festering desire in the first place. Maybe the kid \n\n3. But not others, to be clear — one must always sprinkle in some good old fashioned hypocrisy with undefined ego things\n\n4. Ugh, I can feel you raising an eyebrow. LOOK — I get it. I was brought up this way. I see the logic of it. Celebrating despite having “done” nothing to merit it except staying alive is kinda actually the purest and most life affirming way of relating that there is. Since becoming a mother, my son’s birthdays are both intensely satisfying moments and, equally so, emotionally distraught in myriad ways as the person whose body did the work of bringing him into the world and guarding his form to grow into something that will, by now, after years of constant care and attention, survive and probably even thrive completely on its own for a few hours, and, if circumstances were dire, with a little resourcefulness, much longer than that.\n\nI’m fully aware that none of my “deep-down” wariness may have any actual merit because in the end, most of my own birthdays historically have been absolute shit, and why should my Totally Fair emotional memory give to others what it feels that I myself will never have? It makes sense. It’s not great, but it makes sense. I give my life to my kid, I forego having “his” day to have emotions about my own contribution to his existence to celebrate him. On my own birthday, I now often think of my own mother, that I would love to be able to celebrate her instead of myself, but then… my birthday never really works out for anyone involved. My mother has been on the receiving end of my birthday blues far too often in the past. If I can just spare her the pain of seeing her child upset about being born, that’s the least I can do. I can hope that someday, I will have transcended this can o worms at least so that I’ll find a way to simply celebrate myself the way it works for me, so that I can also celebrate what she did for me.\n</draft>\n\nRespond with JSON only:\n{\n  "edited_text": "the full text with only mechanical fixes applied",\n  "changes": [\n    {"original": "...", "fixed": "...", "reason": "typo/punctuation/clarity"}\n  ],\n  "editorial_note": "1-2 sentences about the piece — be direct, be decisive, say if it\'s good",\n  "questions": ["1-3 specific, concrete questions about the text that might prompt a quick edit. Not \'have you considered restructuring\' — more like \'the last paragraph shifts to second person but the rest is first — intentional?\' or \'who is X? a reader won\'t know.\' Only ask if there\'s genuinely something to ask. An empty list is fine if the piece is clean."]\n}', '--output-format', 'json']' timed out after 120 seconds
